  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Sida Fair Finance Ghana 24 27Oxfam Ghana</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Fair Finance Ghana will add a financial sector angle to current initiatives on tax justice, economic governance, gender and women&apos;seconomic empowerment in the extractive, energy, and agriculture value chains. Crude oil and solid minerals (gold) make up more than60% of Ghana&#x2019;s exports. However, the country&#x2019;s share of revenues from these resources is hardly commensurate with the scale of generated wealth, due to illicit financial flows87and unfavourable tax agreements and practices.
Cocoa is a major earner for Ghana. Cocoa beans purchases in Ghana are financed through syndicated loans, mostly from foreign commercial banks and local counterparts. Foreign banks play an important role, and their influence can be leveraged for better wages and living incomein the cocoa sector.

Fair Finance Ghana will engage in: (1) Capacity development of CSOs and CBOs on sustainable, equitable and well-regulated financial institutions and companies. (2) Awareness creation in community and legal rights and entitlements for CBOs related to FPIC and fair compensation. (3) Public interest and strategic litigation support to affected communities for redress to rights violations. (4) Media andinvestigative journalism. (5) Legal literacy for CBOs in mining host communities/enclaves. (6) Policy dialogue and engagement with companies, finance institutions and government regulators. (7) Community-led evidence gathering of rights violations by companies.
Case studies are planned on the impact for communities of gold mines and aplanned lithium mine. Policy assessments will be carried out, covering banks who are active both in Ghana and Nigeria.
The Ghana Fair Finance Coalition consists of the following organizations: Oxfam in Ghana, Africa Centre for Energy Policy, Media Foundation for West Africa, Ghana Anti-Corruption Coalition, Economic Governance Platform, Ghana Cocoa Civil Society Platform (SEND Ghana), WACAM, Friends of the Nation, Centre for Public Interest Law, Womenin Law and Development in Africa, Peasant Farmers Association of Ghana, Ghana Trades Union Congress, Women in Mining.</narrative>
  </description>
  <participating-org ref="GB-CHC-202918" role="4" type="90">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Oxfam GB Ghana</narrative>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Oxfam is an international confederation organizations working together in more than 90 countries. Oxfam International was formed in1995 by a group of independent non-governmental organizations. Their aim was to work together for greater impact on the international stage to reduce poverty and injustice. The name Oxfam comes from the Oxford Committee for Famine Relief, founded in Britain in 1942. The group campaigned for food supplies to be sent through an allied naval blockade to starving women and children in enemy-occupied Greece during the Second World War.
As well as becoming a world leader in the delivery of emergency relief, Oxfam International implements long-term development programs in vulnerable communities. We are also part of a global movement, campaigning with others,for instance, to end unfair trade rules, demand better health and education services for all, and to combat climate change.

Oxfam GB is an actor in the implementation of this project. Oxfam aims to bring lasting solutions for
quality access to water, poverty, hunger, and injustice in the extractive sector. Oxfam focusses on bridging the gap
between progress made in the north and south, sustainable agricultural livelihoods, free universal quality health care, and promotingaccountable governance and transparency in naturalresource revenue management</narrative>
  </participating-org>
